            <ArticleTitle>Prolonged systemic delivery of tirofiban in a thrombus-laden saphenous vein graft.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>92-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Results from previous trials have shown conflicting results from local delivery of thrombolytic agents to diminish thrombus burden before intervention in native coronary arteries and saphenous vein grafts. We described a patient with an acute coronary syndrome who was treated for 24 hours with systemic tirofiban (Aggrastat), a glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitor, for the treatment of a degenerated saphenous vein graft with a TIMI grade 4 thrombus (large-sized thrombus). Angiographic evaluation 48 hours later revealed complete resolution of the thrombus with normal coronary blood flow.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
